<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- TemporalWindow — ONE mode's settings panel. Hosted in its own OS window by WindowManager (the native
     caption is the only chrome), so there is no panel frame: the root is the dark surface, stretched to fill
     the window.

       ┌─ NowCast ──────────────────────────────────────[pin]─┐
       │  NowCast                                             │  ← the mode's name, from Mode
       │  (the window is sized to fit this — no scroller)     │
       │  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────┐    │
       │  │                                              │    │
       │  │   that mode's body — ONE UserControl from     │    │
       │  │   Controls/Composites, built by ApplyMode     │    │
       │  │   (PastCastTab / NowCastTab / ForeCastTab)    │    │
       │  │                                              │    │
       │  └──────────────────────────────────────────────┘    │
       └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

     ⚠️ ONE CLASS, THREE WINDOWS. This is registered three times (MainWindow), once per TemporalMode, each
     with its own id, title, size and open flag. The alternative — three near-identical window classes — is
     three copies of one layout waiting to drift apart, which is the thing this repo keeps deleting.
     The BODIES keep their old file names (PastCastTab / NowCastTab / ForeCastTab) even though there are no
     tabs any more: they are the same three controls, unchanged, and renaming them would be churn.

     WHAT THIS REPLACED, and why: ONE window with the three modes as TABS, opened by a shared three-dot key.
     That existed because the three mode keys each owned a window and so meant two things at once — "turn the
     mode on" when unlit, "flip my window" when lit — with the window's open state visible nowhere. The keys
     are SPLIT now (Controls/Primitives/SplitTemporalToggle): the square runs the mode, the rail at its foot
     latches this window, and both states are on screen. That fixes the overloading without collapsing the
     windows, which is what buys back the tabs' one real cost — NowCast and ForeCast coexist as modes, so
     their panels can be parked on two monitors again.
     ⚠️ Do NOT re-absorb these into a tabbed panel. See MapViewModel's temporal region before trying.

     ⚠️ THIS WINDOW CANNOT OUTLIVE ITS MODE. Its rail can only be clicked while the mode runs, and
     MapViewModel.OnTemporalModesChanged closes the window when the mode stops — so there is no "every tab
     greyed" state to design for and no empty state to show. The panel is only ever up when it has something
     to act on, which is why nothing in these bodies needs to check whether its mode is running.
     ⚠️ A BODY NEVER STARTS ITS MODE — that is the key's job, and it always was. The date/start/window fields
     drive a replay that is already armed, the watch/warning toggles overlay a live loop, the day/product
     selectors pick which outlook is drawn.

     ⚠️ THE BODY IS BUILT IN CODE, not declared here behind x:Load. Only one of the three is ever wanted per
     window, Mode never changes after construction, and building the one asked for is both cheaper and
     plainer than loading and hiding three. It also sidesteps x:Load's real hazard: bound to a bool it
     UNLOADS again when that bool goes false, which is a trap for any body holding live widget state.

     ⚠️ THE PIN IS WIRED IN CODE-BEHIND for the same reason: which on-top flag it drives depends on Mode,
     which an x:Bind path cannot express. See the pin section of the code-behind. -->
<UserControl x:Class="Anvil.Controls.Windows.TemporalWindow"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:ctl="using:Anvil.Controls.Primitives"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             mc:Ignorable="d">

    <!-- Dark surface fills the whole window. -->
    <Grid Background="{ThemeResource OverlayBarSurfaceBrush}">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/> <!-- title -->
            <!-- ⚠️ Star, and it still measures correctly for sizeToContent: a star row given infinite
                 available height behaves as Auto, so the window's measured height is title + body. Star
                 rather than Auto so the body also FILLS the window if the user drags it larger. -->
            <RowDefinition Height="*"/>    <!-- body -->
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <!-- The mode's own name, in the same vocabulary as its bar key. Filled by ApplyMode. -->
        <TextBlock x:Name="TitleText" Grid.Row="0" Margin="16,14,16,0"
                   FontSize="15" FontWeight="SemiBold" CharacterSpacing="40"
                   Foreground="{ThemeResource TextFillColorSecondaryBrush}"/>

        <!-- ⚠️ NO ScrollViewer. The window is sized to this content instead (WindowManager's
             sizeToContent), so there is nothing to scroll — a scroller here would only ever show a bar that
             could not move. If a body ever outgrows the monitor the manager clamps the window and the
             bottom rows become unreachable: that is the signal to put a scroller back, not to raise the
             clamp. -->
        <ContentPresenter x:Name="BodyHost" Grid.Row="1" Margin="16,14,16,14"/>

        <!-- Pin: keep this window on top. Bound in code-behind — the flag it drives depends on Mode. -->
        <ctl:PinToggle x:Name="Pin"/>
    </Grid>
</UserControl>
